Title: THE CHRONICLES OF THE GUARDIANS
Episode: 106, "THE FIFTH STONE"
Credit: Draft 1 SHORT — PROPOSED, not approved
Author: Prepared for Ω SYD OMEGA 91717 STUDIOS GLOBAL under the founder's direction
Draft date: 2026-11-07
Notes: Cold open and three condensed acts, about 14 script pages of a 50-minute episode. Canon this draft respects: Malachar ends the episode with exactly two stones, Time (amber, which he has had since he woke) and Energy (the dull red of old brick), as the film's sanctum shows; he fails at Karth Vael, so Creation is still in the dry fountain's basin when the film reaches it; Karth Vael looks as the film describes it (a city of fine ash that rebuilds itself and falls, "a city that built itself"); Creation does not speak to Malachar and Liora does not appear to him here, so the film's courtyard scene in Act III is the first time he meets her again; Auren is not in the episode (Locks 3 and 4); Torin joins Lyra's project in person and gives her Kharesh, the name the film says is in the old Warden texts; Seraphine and Malachar do not meet (Lock 2). Anselm Crowe is a proposal. That the word Kharesh is cut into the edge glyphs of the Anvar slab is stated here for the first time (episode 102 shows only the lattice). The episode title counts the stones Malachar has reached for, not the stones he holds. Frequency motif: 9.1717 Hz. Nothing here is filmed, released or playable.

FADE IN:

INT. A COUNTRY HOUSE — A COLD STUDY — DAWN

A study that has been lived in alone for a very long time: a desk heaped with catalogues, glass cases of coins and bone buttons and lacquered boxes, forty clocks all ticking a little apart. Frost on the inside of the window. Every other surface in the room is cold.

At the desk, in a cardigan and fingerless gloves, sits ANSELM CROWE, seventy-nine, dry, tidy, a man who has outlived everyone who might have laughed at his collection. On a saucer by his elbow sits a stone the size of a fist, translucent, the dull red of old brick. The candle beside it leans toward it, flame and all, like a plant toward a window.

The saucer is warm. The room is cold. Crowe puts his gloved hand over the stone the way you cover a cup of tea, and a low note rises under the house.

CROWE
(to the stone, fondly, as to a cat)
Not tonight, old girl. I've the accounts.

SMASH CUT TO:

MAIN TITLES — NINE CONCENTRIC RINGS DRAW THEMSELVES IN LIGHT: SEVEN INSCRIBED, TWO LEFT EMPTY. THE NOTE UNDER THEM IS 9.1717 Hz.

ACT ONE

EXT. THE EDGE OF THE ASH — DAY

SUPER: "KARTH VAEL — A CITY THAT BUILT ITSELF."

A grey horizon where a green one should be. The land goes soft and pale and ends, without a fence, at a shore of fine dust, and beyond it a city stands. Towers, bridges, terraces, all the colour of ashes, all pressed from ash so fine that they seem to be dissolving as you watch. Wind lifts it from the rooftops. Where the wind passes, a wall crumbles. Where the ash settles, a new wall rises, brittle, translucent, grey.

Two vehicles at the edge. CORVIN HALE, forty-eight, heavy, scarred about the neck, sits on a bumper with a flask and a scarf over his mouth. He is not going in. Nobody in his right mind goes in.

Two figures stand where the ground turns to dust. VESPER LUND, thirty-five, sharp, bareheaded, a radio at her hip. And MALACHAR, in a plain grey coat, looking forty, looking at the city the way a man looks at a house he was born in.

MALACHAR
(quietly)
I drew the east bridge.

VESPER
Sir?

MALACHAR
There. The long one, over the canal. I drew it on the back of a bill for lamp oil. It's been trying to build it for four thousand years, and it still gets the third arch wrong.

They watch a span of ash lift from the far bank, reach toward them, one arch, two, a third that sags, and fall into the dust.

VESPER
(very carefully)
Why did they use Creation here alone?

MALACHAR
(a small, tired smile)
Because they did not yet know that anything with no one to say enough will go on forever. We learned it afterward. The Loom was meant to be the one who says enough.

He steps onto the ash. It takes his weight, and his footprint fills behind him as though no one had come.

INT. KARTH VAEL — THE GREAT PLAZA — DAY

A plaza of dust. In its centre, far off, a dry fountain coated in ash, and in its basin among cracked tiles a point of pale rose-gold light that seems to move from within. The wind around it is still.

They cross toward it. The city notices.

All around them, quietly, with no sound but a sigh like a sheet being drawn off a bed, the ash begins to build. A terrace lifts on their left. A harbour road runs down to a sea of nothing. A white staircase climbs a cliff that was not there. It is a city of the Aurelith, as the Aurelith was on a particular morning, and every stone of it is turned toward the man in the grey coat.

VESPER
(a whisper)
It's building for you.

MALACHAR
(very evenly, not breaking stride)
It builds what is wanted. And it has not met anyone who wants so much.

The ash lifts a courtyard ahead of them. A low wall. A little pale fountain that plays with no water. And beside it, built stone by stone from the dust as they watch, a stair of three steps, no higher than a child's knee.

Malachar stops.

He does not look at the courtyard. It is the hardest thing we have seen him do. He lifts his eyes, deliberately, to a point on the horizon above it, and holds them there, and his hands, at his sides, are shaking.

VESPER
(quietly)
Sir.

MALACHAR
(to the horizon)
If I look, it will build her.

A silence in which the ash waits, courteous and terrible, with all its patience.

MALACHAR (CONT'D)
(a breath)
Creation answers to those who want nothing. I have never wanted nothing in my life. It is the one thing the city cannot ignore.

He turns his back on the courtyard and walks, step by step, without looking at it, the way he came. Vesper stays a second longer, looking at the three small steps, and for the first time her face is not a lieutenant's.

EXT. THE EDGE OF THE ASH — DAY

Corvin on the bumper. He rises as they come out onto solid ground, and sees Malachar's face, and says nothing. He hands over the flask. Malachar drinks. The ash behind them lets the courtyard go, wall by wall, into the dust.

MALACHAR
(very quietly)
It cannot be taken by force. I am too full of wanting. I should have known.

VESPER
(carefully)
Then what do we do?

MALACHAR
(handing back the flask)
The next one. The red one. Anselm Crowe. We'll be quick and we'll be gentle, and we will not leave him cold.

ACT TWO

EXT. A COUNTRY HOUSE — A WALLED GARDEN — NIGHT

A frost-furred lawn, a tall dark house with one window lit. A window with a candle in it, leaning.

Vesper and Corvin cross the grass in dark coats, Corvin carrying a lead-lined case the size of a hatbox like a baby he does not entirely trust. Vesper picks the back door in nine seconds and turns the handle.

INT. THE COUNTRY HOUSE — A COLD STUDY — NIGHT

The clocks tick a little apart. Crowe sits in a high-backed chair by the saucer with a rug over his knees and his chin on his chest. He is asleep. The candle leans toward the stone. The stone is warm.

Vesper crosses to the desk. She has done this before. She lifts the stone off the saucer with both hands, as one lifts a sleeping bird, and sets it in the lead case. The candle flame straightens and stands upright, as if disappointed. The room's cold comes back in at once.

Crowe opens his eyes.

CROWE
(mildly)
I thought it would be nearer midnight.

Corvin takes a step. Vesper lifts a hand without turning, and he stops.

CROWE (CONT'D)
(looking at the case)
You'll want it kept dry. It sulks in the damp.

VESPER
(very quietly)
Mr Crowe, we're not here to hurt you.

CROWE
No. You have the look of people who rather hate this. It's the only warm thing in the house. Did you know that? Forty years. I haven't lit the boiler since my wife died. I sit here and I hear the ticking and I am not cold.

VESPER
(it costs her)
I'm sorry.

CROWE
(a dry smile)
You needn't be, my dear. It never liked me. It liked the room. I think it was waiting to be wanted by somebody who would do something with it.

A silence. Vesper closes the case.

VESPER
(after a moment, to Corvin)
Lift him.

CORVIN
(blinking)
What?

VESPER
Put him in the chair by the hearth. I'll do the fire.

Corvin sets the case at her feet. He lifts the old man in his arms like a child, a very large man holding a very old one, and carries him across the study to the cold grate. Vesper kneels, finds kindling in the basket, and builds a fire with quick hard hands. It takes. The first flame catches. Crowe watches it, and then watches her.

CROWE
(gently)
Thank you. I'd forgotten that was an option.

INT. A COUNTRY HOUSE — THE FRONT HALL — NIGHT

Vesper in the doorway, the lead case in her arms. Corvin has gone ahead and is waiting at the car with the engine running. She stops. Behind her, through the study door, the firelight on a very old man's face.

She says, almost to herself:

VESPER
It was warm.

She goes out into the frost.

ACT THREE

EXT. ANVAR RIDGE — THE SURVEY CAMP — DAY

The windswept dig where Hollis and Tuan found the lattice diagram on a slab. A line of flags, a trench, a tarpaulin, a thermos. Lyra, in a field jacket, argues with a plan table under a flapping sheet.

A car comes up the track and stops at the camp's edge. Out of it steps a man in a grey coat with snow still caught in the hem. He is tired in a way a good tailor cannot hide. TORIN DRAVEN. In his coat pocket, under his hand, a small iron key.

TORIN
Dr Thorne. Torin Draven. Draven Risk Advisory. We've written.

LYRA
(not looking up)
You've signed a very generous contract. I've been meaning to ask what you're for.

TORIN
(a small smile)
Collectors.

HOLLIS
(from the trench, wary, friendly)
Is that a job?

TORIN
In some parts of the world it's an illness.

Lyra straightens, at last. She looks at him as at a coin she cannot place. TUAN, at his array on the far side of the table, glances up from his screen. The line on it twitches once, a tiny sharp spike at nine point one seven one seven, and settles. Tuan looks at Torin. Torin looks at the horizon. The spike does not recur.

LYRA
You know the people who'd stop me.

TORIN
(carefully)
I know that they will find what you are looking for with or without you. I would rather it were someone who digs carefully.

LYRA
(slowly)
And what is it you think I am looking for?

TORIN
A network. On stone. Seven nodes and a centre. Hollis found one slab of it on this ridge. There is another, in a basin a long way south, where nobody has dug, in a place the old Warden texts call Kharesh.

The word sits on the cold air like a coin laid on a table.

HOLLIS
(quietly)
Kharesh.

LYRA
(a whisper)
It's on the edge of the slab, in the glyphs. Tuan transcribed it as a name.

TORIN
(very gently)
It is a place.

She looks at him for a long time. There is nothing in his face but the weariness, and, under it, something that might be an apology.

LYRA
(slowly)
Why would you tell me that?

TORIN
(after a very long beat)
Because someone I respected told me, not long ago, that the way you keep a door is to know who is on the far side of it. I would like to know who is.

She does not trust him. She writes the word in her notebook.

INT. A SANCTUM BENEATH A RUINED ABBEY — NIGHT

Stone vault. Candles. A long table. Seven niches in the wall, each shaped to take a stone. One holds an amber stone, warm as honey: Time. Vesper stands before the others with the lead case open on the table, and Corvin sets down the red stone, brick-dull, into the second niche with both hands like a bishop laying down a chalice.

It lights from within, faintly. Every candle in the room leans toward it.

Malachar stands before the wall. Two niches filled. Five empty. He does not touch either stone. He looks at the gaps.

VESPER
(quietly)
Sir. If the Creation stone cannot be taken...

MALACHAR
(not turning)
Then I have been asking the wrong question for three hundred years.

CORVIN
(cheerfully, still catching his breath)
Which is?

MALACHAR
Not how to take. How to be given.

He looks at the two stones, amber and red, glowing in the dark. A very long silence. Under it, in the stone of the vault, in the candles' small leaning flames, the note rises and holds.

MALACHAR (CONT'D)
(to the room, very quietly)
Then it will have to be given.

CUT TO BLACK.

TAG

INT. A COUNTRY HOUSE — A COLD STUDY — NIGHT

The study as before, the forty clocks ticking a little apart, the desk heaped with catalogues, the empty saucer. A real fire in the grate. Crowe sits close to it in the high chair with the rug over his knees. He is looking at the flames with the flat, startled gentleness of someone who has just discovered how long he has been cold.

On the desk, the candle stands straight up.

CROWE
(to the room, very softly)
Thank you for the warmth.

The fire settles in the grate. Outside, in the garden, in the frost, the footprints lead away.

CLOSING SEQUENCE — THE NINE RINGS FADE. A SINGLE LOW TONE, 9.1717 Hz, HOLDS UNDER THE CREDITS.

THE END OF EPISODE 106 (DRAFT 1 SHORT)
